  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1005 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and storms are spreading into northern portions of\nthe CWA, with some potential for additional strong to severe thunderstorms\nthrough midnight and heavy rain persisting overnight.\n\nLatest WX analysis indicates showers and storms moving into northern\nportions of the CWA. While low level lapse rates and instability\nare weak to marginal at best, shear remains strong at 35-45 kt,\nalong with 0-1 km SRH values to 300-350 m2/s2 analyzed per SPC \nmesoanalysis. The overall severe threat is somewhat uncertain \ngiven the minimal instability, but with the strong shear and low\nlevel SRH, additional strong to severe storms will certainly be\npossible so have extended the Watch through midnight. Probably \nof higher confidence is that additional heavy rain and a hydro \nthreat will eventually develop and persist overnight given the \nPWAT values of 2.00-2.25\". The potential for training/backbuilding\nstorms exists as the front slows overnight into Wednesday/Wednesday\nnight. An initial Flood Watch has been issued from central VA \nto the lower Eastern Shore covering the threat tonight into \nWednesday. This will eventually need to be expanded SE to cover \nthe later Wednesday/Thursday time period.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The threat of severe thunderstorms will linger \ninto Wednesday and the potential for torrential rainfall to \nproduce scattered instances of flash flooding will also increase\nthrough Wednesday night and Thursday.\n\nThe severe threat likely continues into Wednesday, but with the\nhigher probabilities shifted to the south and east ahead of the\naforementioned southward-moving cold front. How convection \nevolves today/tonight will also influence the degree of cloud \ncover and destabilization potential on Wednesday. That said, the\nflow aloft is forecast to remain rather strong (40-50kt at \n500mb). SPC has the Slight Risk outlined for our entire CWA (lvl\n2/5).\n\nDespite the ongoing drought conditions, Wednesday into \nWednesday night is showing an increasingly confident signal for \nlocalized flooding, especially in urban and poor drainage areas.\nWPC has most of the area in a Slight Risk ERO again Wednesday \nwith PW generally 2- 2.4\", and a small Moderate Risk area will \nbe issued for SE VA into NE NC. Additionally, a small Moderate \nRisk area will be introduced including NE NC Thursday. Decent \nflow aloft should keep individual cells moving but training \nstorms on top of the rainfall received today/tonight could cause\nscattered instances of flash flooding. A Flood Watch will \neventually be needed for SE VA and NE NC based on the latest \ndata. All total, the QPF forecast through Thursday has generally\n3-5\", with locally higher amounts of rain from Richmond and \nEmporia eastward to SE MD and NE NC.\n\nSome drier air finally moves back into northern portions of the\nregion Thursday and Friday but some showers and storms will \nremain possible, mainly across the southern and southeastern \nportions of the area. Trending drier with below average \ntemperatures by the weekend.",
